  "pages": [
    {
      "name": "intro",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "html",
          "name": "intro_overview",
          "html": "<div class=\"survey-intro\"><img src=\"/img/wy-roadless-areas.png\" alt=\"Wyoming Roadless Areas\" class=\"survey-intro__image\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;margin-bottom:1rem;\"><h3>Purpose</h3><p>This survey is designed to help Wyoming voters learn the issue and share input on what standards should guide decisions about inventoried roadless areas on National Forest System lands in Wyoming.</p><p>The goal is to measure public input, not persuade toward a preferred answer.</p><p><a class=\"button button--small button--secondary\" href=\"/assets/wy_roadless_areas_sources_background.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sources &amp; background</a> <a class=\"button button--small button--secondary\" href=\"/assets/wy_roadless_areas_purpose_privacy_method.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Purpose, privacy &amp; method</a></p><h3>Short educational background</h3><p>Inventoried roadless areas are National Forest lands identified as largely undeveloped and generally without permanent roads.</p><p>The 2001 Roadless Rule generally limited new road construction, road reconstruction, and timber harvest in these areas, with limited exceptions.</p><p>Wyoming has about 3.26 million acres of inventoried roadless areas on National Forest System lands.</p><p>A current federal rulemaking process is considering rescission of the 2001 national rule. A separate bill, H.R. 7695, would make the 2001 rule have no force or effect and require certain permanent and temporary roads where deemed necessary.</p><p>Supporters of keeping roadless protections emphasize water, wildlife habitat, hunting, fishing, recreation, solitude, and long-term public-land inheritance.</p><p>Supporters of changing the rule emphasize wildfire risk, forest health, insect damage, emergency access, timber work, grazing access, local economies, and local management flexibility.</p><h3>Plain-language definitions</h3><dl><dt><strong>Inventoried roadless area</strong></dt><dd>A National Forest area identified through Forest Service inventories as largely undeveloped and generally without permanent roads.</dd><dt><strong>Roadless-area protection</strong></dt><dd>A rule or plan limit on new roads, road reconstruction, or timber harvest in an inventoried roadless area, subject to exceptions.</dd><dt><strong>Temporary road</strong></dt><dd>A road built for a specific project or emergency purpose that is intended to be closed or removed after use.</dd><dt><strong>Permanent road</strong></dt><dd>A road intended to remain available for long-term access or management.</dd><dt><strong>Wildland-urban interface</strong></dt><dd>A place where homes, communities, or infrastructure are close to wildland vegetation and may face wildfire risk.</dd><dt><strong>Watershed</strong></dt><dd>An area of land where water drains into a common stream, river, reservoir, or aquifer.</dd></dl><h3>How responses will be used</h3><ul><li>Results will be reported in aggregate.</li><li>Exact question wording, answer choices, collection dates, sample size, verification method, and revision notes will be published with results.</li><li>Verified Wyoming voter responses and unverified responses will be reported separately when verification is used.</li><li>Individual answers remain confidential.</li></ul></div>"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "familiarity",
      "title": "Section 1: Familiarity",
      "elements": [
        {
          "type": "radiogroup",
